A||||12||saleenfiero||Need opinions on certain other cars to compare to fiero||||3||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||04-30-2001||10:08 PM||||I've need peoples opinions and experiences on some of the following cars.  Just out of curiosity how do these cars rate compared to, oh, I don't know, the fiero.  Need opinions on stuff like acceleration, handling, skidpad, funfactor, and even utility factor.  I got opionions on all of them too, but I want real facts from people who have driven the cars.  This is for a future website.<BR>3rd Gen Camaros 2.8 and Z28's<BR>4th Gen Cam's and Firebird's<BR>Fox Mustangs<BR>Any rice at all<BR>Cavaliers<BR>Sunfires<BR>Early 90's BMW M cars<BR>2001 MR Spyders<BR>Any other cars would be appreciated too<BR><P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||3
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||04-30-2001||11:36 PM||||My 86 Trans Am (305 HO 5 speed) felt more connected. When you wanted the car to do something, it did it right away. (well, as fast as it could anyway) Fieros seem to take longer to respont to pedal and shifter movements. The T/A had power steering so the faster turning response is explained. But I don't really think it was faster. It is the reason I will NEVER own another car with T-tops though. I might own another Firebird someday, I would recommend one to someone who wanted a sports car with a backseat too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Predator||05-01-2001||08:22 AM||||I had a 1995 Trans AM, 4 speed auto 285 hp 350. It ran great, and handled great. It would have outrun my Fiero pretty easy, but this is comparing a 1995 Sports car to a 1985 Sports Car. I would hope it would outperform it. With the turbo my Fiero would give it a run for the money. The only true comparison is to rate cars of the same year, or along the same cost. You have that bang for the buck factor to consider. My Fiero in the mint shape it is in is bearly worth $5,000. That is about as much as I spent on the turbo setup alone. It all really depends on how much you want to spend. You can build a street terror Fiero if you have the cash, but off the line stock it is a good performer, and unique car. That's about it. The Trans Am cost me $16,000. Don't get me wrong I love my Fiero, but the TA was more comfortable with plenty of power, and being a hatchback carried a lot more stuff. The only advantage my Fiero would have is gas mileage.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||05-01-2001||03:26 PM||||I compaire my Fiero to ... Yugo, Plym Aspire, Ford Pinto, AMC Pacer, Gremlin I'm sure there is a few more I'm leaving out.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-01-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000004||GarethWright||05-01-2001||10:27 PM||||My dad has a '97 Sunfire GT that I drive on occasion I have an '87 Fiero GT.  The Fiero is a lot more responsive handling, and seems to "stick" to the road better, the Sunfire has marginally better rear visibility, but the Fiero is much clearer looking forward, they are about the same looking over the shoulder.  The Fiero sunroof is wider than the Sunfires's.  LOTS more trunk space in the Sunfire  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  The passenger compartment is a little narrower in the Sunfire without the gas tank between front seat occupants to seperate them. Sunfire seats are higher as is the rest of the car.  Sunfires come in a 4-door version (yuk  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)  Over all I like the Fiero more for the sports car feel, but I take the Sunfire when I have lots of luggage.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-02-2001||11:09 AM||||Ok maybe this will sound weird. I just bought my first fiero, and 85 SE with V6/auto/removable sunroof.<P>  The other car I was considering was a 82 to 88 VW convertible (rabbit). I had a base model rabbit I bought brand new in 84 and drove till it had 160 miles. It was one of the cars VW made in PA.<P>  The convertibles were always made in Germany, and tended to be higher quality vehicles, and were closer in performance to the GTI models of the rabbit.<P>  When I put the wider/lower profile tires on my base model rabbit it handled very nicely. The car was easy to work on, which was seldom required anyway. I think for the whole 160k miles I replaced the water pump, the shocks, brakes once, and the CAT.<P>  Maybe it sounds odd that someone would either want a fiero or a rabbit convertable, but for a summer car to drive around town and have fun with, you should be able to get a respectable one for $2k to $2.5k.<P>  I simple happened to find a fiero I wanted first. But if I do happen across a good deal on the rabbit convertable... how many project cars are we allowed to own?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-02-2001||11:14 AM||||BTW, I did read that when the 84 fieros came out, one of the painfull realities that smacked pontiac in the face was that the 84 rabbit had better 0 to 60 and quarter mile performance than the 4 cylinder fiero did (maybe it was the Rabbit GTI, not sure on that).<P> there is also alot you can do to a rabbit engine to soup it up, carbs, headers, cams. <P>Pontiac fixed the 'problem' with the V6 the following year.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||05-02-2001||12:18 PM||||Ken, since the GTI was the high performance model (at least I assume it is, I'm not much if a VW fan myself) and the '84 Fiero was without a performance model. They weren't really in the same class.<P>84Bill, It's people like you that make people like me want to make fun of iron duke owners.<P>Saleen, I have also (once or twice) driven a 1987 2.8 Firebird, man are those things slow. They would be great if someone put a 3.8 in them though. I didn't see that you wanted to compare them to the fiero also ir I would said that earlier.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||05-02-2001||12:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B><P>84Bill, It's people like you that make people like me want to make fun of iron duke owners.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thats why I have such a good sense of humor otherwise I'd be a V6 Fiero owner : <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">:<P>I have not driven any car that comes close to the way a Fiero handels (up yours C&D mag). There is just no description except "sublime" perhaps a porshe would be a close match but I doubt it. Fieros ar in a class of their own. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||05-02-2001||02:52 PM||||I think the stock 84 rabbits were around 65HP and the GTIs were around 90 - 95HP, so for the HP and weight I think you are right, most likely it was the GTI.<P> but Pontaic was still embarassed just the same.<P>Im not sure what you mean by saying the 84 fiero was not a performance model. When you release a new production 2 passenger, rear engine car that looks like a certain Italian sports car, and whos name sounds like a certain Italian sports car, Im pretty sure the 84 2.5 Fiero was suppose to be the performance version.<P>   I think the V6 in 85 was an Ohkrap response. These kinds of things happen all the time at big car companies. When GM made the EV1, the battery division kept saying for 3 years that the batteries would loose half their capacity below 35°F, and the rest of the project engineers ignored them.<P> Then when the car was ready for release (production started) they realised you could only drive the thing about 30 miles on a full charge below 32°. They then scrambled to address the problem, and ended up only marking the cars in california and arizona.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||KRMFiero||05-03-2001||07:57 AM||||It was originally comapred to the CRX and MR2..<P>btw. my friend just got a CRX, i dont like it at all but he has 4 15's in it it hits hard...   car does have some power to it (well that high redline anyway)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Pontiaddict||05-03-2001||08:20 PM||||The V6 was planned before the car was put into production. Then again, the 88 suspension was designed for the Fiero in the late 70's. The 84 was the "Build them cheap so lots of people can afford to buy them and pay for the costs of designing the car." year. Also, GM wasn't too thrilled about building the car in the first place, so compromises were made to get it even produced. The 84's were rushed into production and they didn't have time to do any fine tuning to the car with all of the changes they had to make, much less build a performance model. In 85 they improved on things, revising the suspension and adding the V6 to the linup.||||reg||1
Z||000012||LottaBallsCamaro||05-03-2001||11:08 PM||||Guess what I'm going to compare the fiero to...My 91RS of course! I dont have to much experience with fieros, but from the little driving I've done in them I do know that the fiero should handle better but is alot harder to drive (my firends seems to have a worn out suspension). The camaro is hellua more comfortable and is more a point and shoot car, forgiving as long as you know when to let off the go pedal. Build quality is about the same, but I like the camaro interior(91-92)not the previous years, which is ugly, better then the fiero. At high speed, the camaro is stabler but the fiero is faster (then my 305, but cant hang with any of the 350's). The 2.8 camaros dont have enough power to get out of there own way, and then if you are lucky to find one of the ironduke equipped ones, you'll be lucky to accelerate up a hill. The small block is more reliable in my opinion and alot easier to work on(more engine compartmet room). They both have the cool factor though and both have the whitetrash stigma(like every 80's american car). My uncle just bought a new mr2, and that thing corners like a gocart, it also has variable valve timing, and only costs 20,000, But It sucks anyway, I hate jap cars. <p>[This message has been edited by LottaBallsCamaro (edited 05-03-2001).]||||reg||1
